                <section class="dics-kb-section py-5 bg-white border-bottom">
                    <div class="dics-container container px-3 px-sm-4">
                        <div class="dics-kb-section-head mb-4">
                            <span class="dics-kb-eyebrow">ARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENCES</span>
                            <h2 class="h2 fw-bold mb-2">Where Odoo and SAP Fundamentally Diverge</h2>
                            <p class="text-secondary mb-0">
                                These are the structural differences that determine long-term cost, agility, and risk — not feature checklists.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="dics-kb-pillars d-grid gap-3">
                            <article>
                                <span>01</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3 class="h5 fw-bold mb-2">Implementation Timeline &amp; Time-to-Value</h3>
                                    <p>
                                        Odoo projects routinely go live in 3–6 months with a phased approach. SAP S/4HANA programmes commonly run 14–24 months before meaningful production use, driven by heavy process redesign, data migration complexity, and extensive testing cycles.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </article>
                            <article>
                                <span>02</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3 class="h5 fw-bold mb-2">Licensing Model &amp; Cost Predictability</h3>
                                    <p>
                                        Odoo Enterprise uses a simple flat per-user model that includes virtually all applications. SAP combines named-user licences, engine metrics, and “Indirect Access” rules that frequently create unexpected cost spikes years after go-live.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </article>
                            <article>
                                <span>03</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3 class="h5 fw-bold mb-2">Customisation &amp; Upgrade Risk</h3>
                                    <p>
                                        Odoo’s modular inheritance model (Python + OWL) allows clean extensions that survive upgrades. SAP customisations written in ABAP often create significant technical debt and expensive remediation work during major version moves.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </article>
                            <article>
                                <span>04</span>
                                <div>
                                    <h3 class="h5 fw-bold mb-2">Talent Availability &amp; Maintenance Cost</h3>
                                    <p>
                                        Python developers are widely available and relatively affordable. Experienced ABAP and SAP functional consultants remain scarce and expensive, driving higher long-term support costs for SAP landscapes.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </article>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>




